"Texas Points System Information | El Paso Traffic Ticket Firm": "",
"Note (updated): the Driver Responsibility Program surcharges described below were repealed by the Texas Legislature effective September 1, 2019, and outstanding surcharges were waived. Points are no longer assessed under this program. The article is kept for reference because convictions still affect your driving record, insurance and license eligibility. Current license and suspension rules are at the": "",
"Texas DPS driver license site": "",
"The Driver Responsibility Program established a system that authorized the Texas Department of Public Safety (DPS) to assess surcharges to an individual based on certain traffic offenses that occurred on or after September 1, 2003. Individuals were notified by mail each time a surcharge was added to their driver record. Surcharges were in addition to other fees and did not replace a suspension, revocation, denial, disqualification or cancellation resulting from the same conviction.": "",
"Surcharges were assessed in two ways: the point system, and conviction-based surcharges. Individuals who had both points and convictions reported to their driver record received separate surcharges for each offense.": "",
"Point System": "",
"Points were assessed for traffic convictions. Once the conviction was added to the driver record, points were assigned and remained on the record for three years from the date of conviction: two points for a Texas or out-of-state traffic conviction, and three points for a conviction that resulted in a crash.": "",
"Points were not assessed for individuals who completed": "",
"Individuals with six or more points were assessed a surcharge every year they maintained six or more points: $100 for the first six points and $25 for each additional point.": "",
"Conviction Based": "",
"Individuals convicted of one of the offenses below paid an annual surcharge for three years from the date of conviction. Points were not assessed for these offenses because the surcharge was automatic upon conviction.": "",
"Type of conviction": "",
"Annual surcharge (3 years)": "",
"1st Driving While Intoxicated (DWI), Intoxication Assault or Manslaughter": "",
"Subsequent DWI": "",
"DWI with blood alcohol concentration of 0.16 or more": "",
"No Insurance": "",
"Driving While License Invalid (DWLI)": "",
"No Driver License (no license, expired license or endorsement violation)": "",
"No Insurance Conviction": "",
"Individuals convicted of No Insurance who had insurance at the time of the offense could submit proof of insurance to DPS. The suspension was waived but the surcharge still had to be paid. (Today, a no-insurance ticket is still one of the most expensive tickets to simply pay — call us before you do.)": "",
"Driver Record Review and Payment": "",
"DPS reviewed surcharge cases annually and used an outside vendor (Municipal Services Bureau) to collect, with added service fees. Surcharges had to be paid within 30 days or the driver license was suspended until an installment agreement was established or the surcharges were paid in full. Indigency and incentive programs reduced surcharges for lower-income drivers. Collected surcharges funded the state trauma centers and the general revenue fund.": "",
"What still matters today": "",
"Convictions stay on your driving record": "",
"and are what insurers rate you on. Avoiding the conviction — through dismissal, deferred disposition or a driving safety course — is still the goal.": "",
"Occupational licenses": "",
"are still available for drivers whose license is suspended; see the": "",
"DPS driver license pages": "",
"Unpaid tickets still become": "",
"and can block license renewal and vehicle registration.": "",
"How the Texas Driver Responsibility Program points and surcharges worked, what was repealed in 2019, and why avoiding a conviction still matters for your record.": "",
